You MUST submit a `development_result` artifact before declaring completion: a markdown document with frontmatter `type: development_result` and `status: completed|partial`, then `## Summary` (exactly one item), `## Files Changed` (one item per file), and the proof sections below. Each proof item's bracketed ID is the reference; the item text is the evidence.

**## Plan Items Proven** (required when the execution plan has steps, including steps grouped into named subplans): one `- [S-N] <proof>` item per plan step, where `S-N` EXACTLY matches the step's stable ID from the plan document and the proof cites concrete evidence, e.g. `- [S-1] Modified src/validation.py lines 10-25; pytest tests/test_validation.py -q passes.`

**## Plan Items Proven** (when the plan explicitly uses `## Work Units` instead): in the main integration session, one `- [unit-ID] <proof>` item for EVERY work unit in the plan; each bracketed ID EXACTLY matches the plan's `[unit-ID]` bracket ID. Isolated worker prompts override this with exactly one proof for their assigned unit. Never mix step IDs and work-unit IDs in one result.

**## Analysis Items Addressed** (required when ANALYSIS FEEDBACK exists): one `- [<ID>] <proof>` item per localized `What Came Up Short` finding from the prior analysis artifact, where the ID EXACTLY matches that entry's stable ID (e.g. `DA-001`) and the proof cites how it was resolved.

Each proof ID must be distinct — duplicates fail validation. Unknown IDs (not matching a plan step ID, work-unit bracket ID, or analysis item ID) also fail validation and trigger a re-prompt.

Every requirement above applies to `status: completed`. A `status: partial` result is free-form below the frontmatter — no section or proof is required — so submit one the moment you cannot finish rather than forcing a completion claim you cannot prove. Include `## Next Steps` (the remaining work) and `## Continuation` (your session id): they are not required, but they are what lets the next iteration resume your work.
